The transverse momentum resolution of primary particles tracks fit through the primary vertex is shown in Fig. 47. For transverse momentum above 1 GeV/c, it is more difficult to resolve the curvature of the track. This means that the momentum relative error increases for high \(p_{T}\). At low momenta, the resolution is affected by the energy loss in the TPC. Due to their smaller energy these low momentum particles lose a significant proportion of their energy in the TPC, do not travel through the whole volume in the TPC, and therefore their tracks are shorter. The momentum measurement error increases as a consequence. Finally, the energy loss in the TPC is dependent not only on the particle momentum, but also on the mass of the particle, so that there are differences in the resolution for different particles at low momenta, as figured in Fig. 47 for antiprotons and pions. The best relative momentum resolution for pions is 2% at \(p_{T}=0.5\) GeV/c and it is increasing with \(p_{T}\) up to 10%.

